      "content": "While tetanus immunization provides excellent protection, leaving a wound open for extended periods can still risk infection, especially with gardening tools involved. Even with your vaccination, it's not absolutely safe to assume that you won't need medical attention because of potential gaps in your tetanus immunization or other possible contaminants in your environment. I can appreciate wanting to avoid unnecessary medical costs, but if there are any concerns such as signs of infection (increased pain, redness, swelling, warmth, pus or fever), it's best to see a healthcare provider promptly. Self-treatment with antiseptics and ibuprofen can help, but it doesn't replace professional medical care. Keep monitoring the wound closely for any signs of complications. If in doubt, seeking a healthcare professional's advice is always the best course of action for peace of mind.",
